#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:2
msgid "Sampling design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:3
msgid ""
"*Plan and export random or systematic sample locations, with optional "
"stratification*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:6
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:8
msgid ""
"The **Sampling design** recipe creates point locations for collecting "
"reference observations. Use it to:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:10
msgid "define the area where samples may be placed;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:11
msgid "divide the area into strata, when needed;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:12
msgid "plan how many samples to collect;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:13
msgid "distribute the samples among the strata;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:14
msgid "choose random or systematic sample placement; and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:15
msgid ""
"export the sample locations to a Google Earth Engine (GEE) table asset or"
" the SEPAL workspace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:17
msgid ""
"The recipe creates the sampling design only. It does not label the sample"
" locations, assess map accuracy or calculate final area estimates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:19
msgid ""
"Sampling Design does not show an interactive preview because generating "
"and validating the sample locations can exceed interactive-map limits. "
"The locations are created through an export. A completed GEE table asset "
"can later be added to the map from the **Layers** panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:23
msgid ""
"A sampling design can be valid but still be a poor fit for the study. "
"Consider what must be estimated, how much uncertainty is acceptable, how "
"many locations can be assessed reliably with the available time and "
"resources, and how the results will be calculated. Consult a sampling "
"specialist when the results will support official statistics or other "
"high-impact decisions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:26
msgid "Terms used in this recipe"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:28
msgid ""
"**Area of interest (AOI)**: the outer geographic boundary within which "
"samples may be selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:29
msgid ""
"**Stratum**: a non-overlapping part of the eligible sampling area, "
"commonly defined by a map class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:30
msgid ""
"**Target reporting category**: the category the design is mainly intended"
" to estimate, such as forest or forest loss. It can differ from the "
"strata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:31
msgid ""
"**Reference observation**: information recorded for a sample, such as "
"whether forest or forest loss is present. It may come from visual "
"interpretation of high-resolution satellite imagery, a field inventory or"
" another suitable source."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:32
msgid ""
"**Anticipated proportion**: a planning estimate of how common the target "
"reporting category is across the AOI and, when stratified, within each "
"stratum. It is prior information, not a result measured from the sample."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:33
msgid "**Sample allocation**: the number of samples assigned to each stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:34
msgid ""
"**Sample arrangement**: the method used to place the samples within each "
"stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:37
msgid "Sampling theory in brief"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:39
msgid ""
"Sampling means observing selected locations to learn about a larger area "
"or population without visiting every place. How those locations are "
"chosen affects what conclusions the sample can support."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:41
msgid ""
"This recipe covers one part of a study: it decides how many locations to "
"select and where to place them. Exporting the points does not finish the "
"study. Reference observations must still be collected or interpreted at "
"those locations and analysed in a way that accounts for how the locations"
" were selected and allocated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:43
msgid ""
"Anticipated proportions, calculated sample sizes and displayed "
"uncertainty estimates are planning values, not results measured from the "
"collected observations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:46
msgid "Workflow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:48
msgid "Create a sampling design in the following order:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:50
msgid "Select the AOI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:51
msgid "Choose an unstratified or stratified design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:52
msgid ""
"Anticipate the target proportion, when useful prior information is "
"available."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:53
msgid "Plan the sample count and its allocation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:54
msgid "Choose random or systematic sample placement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:55
msgid "Export the sample locations and review the result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:57
msgid ""
"Changing an earlier step may invalidate a later step. An orange error "
"indicator means that the affected panel must be reviewed again before "
"continuing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:60
msgid "Start"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:62
msgid ""
"Go to the **Process** tab and select :btn:`<fa-solid fa-circle-plus> Add "
"recipe` > **Sampling design**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:64
msgid ""
"The setup wizard opens the **Area of interest** and **Stratification** "
"panels. Complete both panels to initialize the recipe. The following "
"parameter buttons then become available in the lower-right corner:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:66
msgid ":guilabel:`STR`: Stratification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:67
msgid ":guilabel:`PRO`: Anticipate proportion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:68
msgid ":guilabel:`ALO`: Sample Allocation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:69
msgid ":guilabel:`ARR`: Sample Arrangement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:71
msgid ""
"Select the :btn:`<fa-solid fa-cloud-arrow-down>` button in the upper-"
"right corner to export a complete design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:78
msgid "Area of interest"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:80
msgid ""
"Use the **Area of interest** panel to define the outer boundary within "
"which sample locations may be selected. The AOI may come from a country "
"or administrative boundary, a drawn geometry, a GEE table, or another "
"source supported by the standard SEPAL AOI selector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:82
msgid ""
"Choose an AOI that covers the area from which sample locations should be "
"selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:84
msgid "Check the AOI before continuing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:86
msgid "Include every location that belongs to the population of interest."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:87
msgid ""
"Avoid unnecessary geometric detail. Very complex boundaries can increase "
"processing time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:90
msgid "Stratification"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:92
msgid ""
"Use the **Stratification** panel to choose whether the sample is divided "
"among map classes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:95
msgid "Why stratify"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:97
msgid ""
"Stratification divides the eligible sampling area into non-overlapping "
"strata before samples are selected. Its main statistical purpose is to "
"improve sampling efficiency. A useful stratification may:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:99
msgid "reduce the expected uncertainty for the same total number of samples; or"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:100
msgid "require fewer samples to reach the same target uncertainty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:102
msgid ""
"These gains are not guaranteed. They are most likely when the strata "
"separate areas with meaningfully different occurrences of the target "
"reporting category and the samples are allocated appropriately. A poor "
"stratification or allocation may provide no gain and can make the result "
"less precise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:104
msgid ""
"The stratification map does not need to show the target reporting "
"category itself. For example, ecological zones can define strata in a "
"design intended to estimate forest area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:107
msgid "Choose the design type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:109
msgid "Use one of the following designs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:111
msgid ""
"**Unstratified**: sample the eligible area inside the AOI without "
"dividing it into strata. Use this option when no useful stratification is"
" available or separate allocation among map classes is unnecessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:112
msgid ""
"**Stratified**: use a categorical image to divide the AOI into strata and"
" allocate samples separately to each."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:115
msgid "Create an unstratified design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:117
msgid ""
"Turn on **Unstratified** to select sample locations across the eligible "
"area inside the AOI without a stratification image or band."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:120
msgid "Create a stratified design"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:122
msgid ""
"A categorical image assigns each pixel to a discrete class, such as a "
"land-cover type or ecological zone. An image can contain one or more "
"bands; select the band containing the class values to use as strata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:124
msgid "For a stratified design:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:126
msgid ""
"Select **Asset** to use a GEE image asset, or **Recipe** to use the image"
" output of another SEPAL recipe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:127
msgid "Select a categorical image."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:128
msgid "Select the band containing the stratum values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:129
msgid ""
"Leave **Scale** blank to let SEPAL choose a suitable value. Enter a value"
" in metres only when the design needs a different resolution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:133
msgid ""
"When **Recipe** is selected, SEPAL evaluates that recipe as part of the "
"stratification calculations and sample export. A processing-intensive "
"recipe can make these operations slow or cause an Earth Engine timeout or"
" memory error. If this happens repeatedly, export the recipe output as a "
"GEE image asset and select it with **Asset**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:135
msgid ""
"Once the required inputs are available, SEPAL automatically starts "
"calculating the area and weight of each stratum within the AOI. Wait for "
"the calculation to finish before continuing. The table displays:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:137
msgid "**Value**: the numeric pixel value identifying the stratum;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:138
msgid "**Area (ha)**: the mapped stratum area in hectares;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:139
msgid "**Weight**: the stratum area divided by the total mapped area; and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:140
msgid "**Total**: the total mapped area and a weight of 100 percent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:147
msgid ""
"Review the calculated strata. Edit a label or colour when the image does "
"not provide suitable class metadata. The labels and colours are included "
"with the exported design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:151
msgid ""
"A masked pixel has no usable value in the selected band and is not part "
"of any stratum. Review the calculated total area and class list before "
"allocating samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:154
msgid "Sampling frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:156
msgid ""
"The sampling frame is the geographic area eligible for sample selection. "
"For an unstratified design, it is the eligible area inside the AOI. For a"
" stratified design, it is the eligible, unmasked pixels belonging to "
"included strata inside the AOI. Locations outside the sampling frame "
"cannot be selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:159
msgid "Scale"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:161
msgid ""
"Scale sets the resolution at which the stratification is read, and with "
"it how much processing the calculations require. For a GEE image asset it"
" starts from the image's own pixel size; see `Grids and projections`_. "
"The approximate number of pixels processed depends on the AOI area "
"divided by the square of the scale. For the same AOI, halving the scale "
"processes approximately four times as many pixels; doubling it processes "
"approximately one quarter as many."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:163
msgid ""
"A smaller scale can preserve more spatial detail when the source image "
"supports that resolution, but it increases processing time and memory "
"use. Choosing a scale finer than the source's useful resolution mostly "
"resamples the same information and adds work. A larger scale is faster, "
"but it can remove small or narrow strata, shift class boundaries and "
"change the calculated area and weight of each stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:165
msgid ""
"The class recorded for each sample is the one found at the sample "
"location at the resolution in use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:168
msgid "Direct and Queued calculations"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:170
msgid ""
"Choose how SEPAL calculates stratum areas. Workload depends on the number"
" of pixels and the computation required for each pixel. AOI area and "
"Scale determine the pixel count; a stored asset adds little processing, "
"while a source **Recipe** may add little or a lot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:172
msgid "Select one of the following options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:174
msgid ""
"**Direct**: start the calculation immediately in GEE's interactive "
"environment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:175
msgid ""
"**Queued**: place the calculation in GEE's batch queue. Batch tasks can "
"run longer and may have more memory than Direct calculations, making them"
" better suited to demanding work. GEE usually runs only a few batch tasks"
" at once, so the calculation may wait for capacity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:177
msgid "Demanding calculations can still be slow or fail with either option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:180
msgid "Anticipate proportion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:182
msgid ""
"Use the **Anticipate proportion** panel to choose the one target "
"reporting category that the design should plan for and describe how "
"common it is expected to be within each stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:184
msgid ""
"The target reporting category is the category whose area or proportion "
"the design is mainly intended to estimate. It does not have to be one of "
"the strata or originate from the stratification map. For example, "
"ecological zones can be the strata while forest is the target reporting "
"category."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:187
msgid "Why anticipate a proportion"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:189
msgid ""
"Anticipated proportions help SEPAL plan where samples are expected to "
"reduce uncertainty most. With an appropriate allocation strategy and "
"credible anticipated proportions, the design may achieve similar expected"
" precision with fewer observations, or better expected precision with the"
" same number of observations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:191
msgid ""
"SEPAL also uses anticipated proportions to estimate how precise the "
"result may be. For a stratified design, you can set a precision target "
"and let SEPAL calculate the total sample size, or set the number of "
"samples you can assess and see the expected precision. For an "
"unstratified design, you enter the number of samples directly and SEPAL "
"can estimate the expected precision. This helps balance the desired "
"precision against the available assessment capacity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:193
msgid ""
"These calculations focus on one target reporting category. Choose the "
"category that matches the main objective. Other categories can still be "
"recorded. Inaccurate anticipated proportions can make the design less "
"efficient, but they do not invalidate the observations collected later. "
"The final precision is calculated from those observations and may differ "
"from the planning result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:196
msgid "Choose how to anticipate proportions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:198
msgid ""
"Select **No proportions** when reliable prior information is unavailable."
" This does not set the proportion to zero or change whether the design is"
" stratified. Otherwise, select one of the following methods:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:201
msgid "Probability image"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:203
msgid ""
"Choose **Probability image** when you have an estimated probability of "
"the target reporting category at each location. SEPAL averages these "
"probabilities within each stratum to approximate the expected proportion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:205
msgid ""
"For example, suppose **Tree** is the target reporting category and a "
"SEPAL classification provides a **Class** band and a **Tree %** "
"probability band. The **Class** band can define land-cover strata, while "
"**Tree %** anticipates the proportion of Tree within each stratum. "
"Alternatively, ecological zones could define the strata while the same "
"**Tree %** band anticipates Tree within each zone."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:207
msgid ""
"Select the probability band for the target category — **Tree %** in this "
"example — not **Class probability**, which describes the confidence of "
"whichever class was assigned at each location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:210
msgid "Categorical image"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:212
msgid ""
"Choose **Categorical image** when a map other than the stratification "
"contains a class corresponding to the target reporting category. Within "
"each stratum, SEPAL calculates the share of the area mapped as that class"
" and uses it as the anticipated proportion."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:214
msgid ""
"For example, suppose **Tree** is the target reporting category. "
"Ecological zones can define the strata, while the **Class** band of a "
"land-cover map supplies the anticipated proportions by selecting **Tree**"
" as the target class: each zone is anticipated to contain the share of "
"trees that map shows in it. Alternatively, a forest map can define the "
"strata while a second forest map, from another source, supplies the "
"proportions: where the two diverge, the stratum is less certain and needs"
" more samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:216
msgid ""
"The stratification itself cannot supply this information: every stratum "
"would be mapped as either entirely the target category or none of it, so "
"no stratum would appear uncertain. The map's class should also mean the "
"same thing as the target reporting category, over about the same period; "
"otherwise the proportions reflect differences between the two maps rather"
" than the uncertainty within each stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:219
msgid "Manual"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:221
msgid ""
"Select **Manual** to enter the anticipated percentage separately for each"
" stratum. Base the values on prior studies, pilot observations or "
"documented expert judgement."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:224
msgid "Configure the panel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:226
msgid "For **Probability image** or **Categorical image**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:228
msgid "Select an **Asset** or **Recipe**, then select the source image and band."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:229
msgid ""
"For a probability image, indicate whether values range from 0 to 100 "
"instead of 0 to 1. For a categorical image, select the target **Class**. "
"When class metadata is unavailable, load the distinct values or enter the"
" numeric class value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:230
msgid "Enter the processing **Scale** and select **Direct** or **Queued**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:232
msgid ""
"The **Direct** and **Queued** options behave as described under `Direct "
"and Queued calculations`_. The Scale in this panel controls the "
"anticipated-proportion calculation's pixel workload independently of the "
"stratification Scale."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:234
msgid ""
"Once the required inputs are available, SEPAL automatically calculates "
"the anticipated proportion for each stratum. Wait for the calculation to "
"finish and review the values before continuing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:236
msgid ""
"For an image-based method, SEPAL combines the calculated stratum "
"proportions using their area weights. If reliable prior information is "
"available for the AOI as a whole, enter **Anticipated overall "
"proportion** to apply that value. SEPAL adjusts the stratum values to "
"match it while keeping every stratum between 0 and 100 percent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:238
msgid ""
"For example, suppose **Tree** is the target reporting category and the "
"probability image produces an anticipated overall proportion of 5 "
"percent. A previous assessment for the same area suggests that the "
"proportion is closer to 10 percent. Enter **10** as the **Anticipated "
"overall proportion**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:240
msgid ""
"SEPAL doubles every calculated stratum proportion. A stratum calculated "
"at 10 percent becomes 20 percent, while one calculated at 2 percent "
"becomes 4 percent. The relative differences among strata are preserved, "
"but their area-weighted overall proportion becomes 10 percent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:242
msgid ""
"Review the calculated or manually entered proportions and any overall "
"adjustment before continuing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:250
msgid "Sample allocation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:252
msgid ""
"Use the **Sample Allocation** panel to plan how many samples the design "
"should contain and, for a stratified design, how they are distributed "
"among the strata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:254
msgid ""
"Anticipated proportions allow SEPAL to estimate precision for an entered "
"sample size. For a stratified design, they also allow SEPAL to calculate "
"the sample size needed for a precision target and use **Optimal** or "
"**Power** allocation. Without anticipated proportions, enter the sample "
"size directly; SEPAL cannot estimate precision for the target reporting "
"category. **Proportional**, **Equal** and **Balanced** allocation remain "
"available for a stratified design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:257
msgid "Why allocation matters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:259
msgid ""
"The total sample size controls the overall assessment effort. When the "
"total is fixed, assigning more samples to one stratum leaves fewer for "
"the others. Two designs with the same total sample size can therefore "
"have different expected precision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:262
msgid "Set the target"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:264
msgid ""
"With anticipated proportions, a stratified design can use one of two "
"targets:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:266
msgid ""
"**Samples**: enter the total number of samples you can assess or have "
"otherwise decided to collect. SEPAL estimates the expected precision for "
"that sample size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:267
msgid ""
"**Error**: enter a target relative margin of error. SEPAL calculates the "
"total sample size expected to meet that target."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:269
msgid ""
"For an unstratified design, enter the sample count directly in the "
"allocation table. When an anticipated proportion is available, SEPAL "
"displays the expected margin of error for that count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:271
msgid ""
"The **Error** target is a relative margin of error: it expresses the "
"planned uncertainty as a percentage of the anticipated overall "
"proportion, not as percentage points. It is a planning result and depends"
" on the anticipated proportions being reasonable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:273
msgid ""
"The **Confidence level** describes how often the calculated margin-of-"
"error range would be expected to contain the true proportion if the "
"sampling process were repeated many times. **95 percent** is the standard"
" choice. Use **90 percent** when somewhat less confidence is acceptable "
"in return for a smaller required sample, or **99 percent** when stronger "
"confidence justifies a larger required sample."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:277
msgid ""
"SEPAL uses the total sample size, whether entered directly or calculated "
"from an **Error** target, to allocate samples among strata. The margin of"
" error, whether entered as the target or estimated from the sample size, "
"is only a planning estimate. How closely it matches the final uncertainty"
" depends largely on how closely the proportions found in the reference "
"observations match the anticipated proportions. Calculate the final "
"uncertainty from the collected observations using a method appropriate "
"for the sampling design."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:280
msgid "Choose an allocation strategy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:282
msgid ""
"The allocation strategy controls how the total sample size is divided "
"among strata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:284
msgid ""
"When comparing strategies, watch the value that SEPAL recalculates. With "
"**Error** selected, a lower sample size indicates greater planned "
"efficiency for the same uncertainty target. With **Samples** selected, a "
"lower margin of error indicates greater planned efficiency for the same "
"sample size. These comparisons depend on the anticipated proportions "
"being credible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:286
msgid "Allocation strategies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:290
#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:382
msgid "Strategy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:291
msgid "How it allocates samples"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:292
msgid "When it may be useful"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:293
msgid "**Proportional**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:294
msgid "Assigns samples in proportion to mapped stratum area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:295
msgid "When similar sampling effort per unit area is appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:296
msgid "**Equal**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:297
msgid ""
"Assigns sample counts as evenly as possible among strata. Counts can "
"differ by one when the total does not divide evenly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:298
msgid ""
"When small strata need strong representation. It can assign many samples "
"to very small strata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:299
msgid "**Balanced**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:300
msgid "Uses the midpoint between equal and proportional allocation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:301
msgid "When a compromise between area and representation is needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:302
msgid "**Optimal**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:303
msgid ""
"Uses anticipated proportions to place more samples in strata expected to "
"contribute more uncertainty."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:304
msgid ""
"When the anticipated proportions are credible and the main goal is "
"efficient estimation of the target reporting category."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:305
msgid "**Power**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:306
msgid ""
"Modifies Optimal by reducing the influence of each stratum's expected "
"target amount. A value of 1 matches Optimal; lower values shift "
"relatively more samples toward smaller non-zero expected amounts."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:307
msgid ""
"When you want to give more representation than Optimal to strata expected"
" to contain smaller amounts of the target reporting category."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:309
msgid ""
"Automatic allocations are rounded to whole samples and then adjusted to "
"preserve the requested total and the minimum per stratum. The resulting "
"allocated per-stratum counts can therefore differ slightly from the ideal"
" mathematical proportions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:317
msgid "Set the minimum per stratum"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:319
msgid ""
"Use **Min samples/stratum** to require automatic allocation to assign at "
"least this many samples to each stratum. The recipe requires at least two"
" samples in every stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:321
msgid ""
"This setting applies to Proportional, Balanced, Optimal and Power "
"allocation. Equal and Manual use the hard minimum of two directly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:325
msgid ""
"SEPAL requires at least two samples per stratum, but this is usually too "
"few to describe a stratum reliably. If results will be reported by "
"stratum, choose a higher minimum based on the required precision and the "
"number of locations that can be assessed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:327
msgid ""
"The total sample size must be large enough to satisfy the minimum for "
"every stratum. Rare strata may not contain enough usable locations to "
"meet the allocated count, even when the arithmetic allocation is valid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:330
msgid "Allocate manually"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:332
msgid ""
"Select **Manual** to enter the sample size for every stratum. Each value "
"must be an integer of at least two."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:335
msgid "Review the allocation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:337
msgid "Review the allocation table. Confirm that:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:339
msgid ""
"the total sample size can be assessed reliably within the available time "
"and resources;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:340
msgid "important and rare strata receive enough samples; and"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:341
msgid "any displayed margin of error is acceptable as a planning estimate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:343
msgid ""
"The allocation sets the requested counts. Whether the export can place "
"them is checked later; see `When too few sample locations are found`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:346
msgid "Sample arrangement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:348
msgid ""
"Use the **Sample Arrangement** panel to place the allocated samples "
"within the AOI or within each stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:351
msgid "Why arrangement matters"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:353
msgid ""
"Arrangement controls spatial coverage and the randomization used to "
"select locations. It does not change the allocation among strata. Choose "
"it based on the intended analysis, spatial pattern of the population, "
"operational constraints and need for reproducibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:355
msgid "Random and systematic arrangements make different trade-offs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:357
msgid ""
"Randomly placed locations can cluster or leave spatial gaps. No minimum "
"separation is enforced."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:358
msgid ""
"Systematic placement spreads locations regularly across space. Reusing "
"the same grid provides stable sample locations for estimates over time "
"and may allow previously collected observations to be retained when the "
"stratification is corrected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:361
msgid "Random arrangement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:363
msgid "Select **Random** to place locations randomly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:366
msgid "Systematic arrangement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:368
msgid "Select **Systematic** to place samples on a regularly spaced grid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:370
msgid ""
"SEPAL places locations in a hexagonally spaced pattern and organizes them"
" into a fixed set of nested **grid levels**. Each level represents an "
"available sampling density. Moving one level coarser removes alternating "
"locations in a fixed pattern, leaving roughly half within each stratum. "
"Moving to a denser level adds those locations back while retaining the "
"locations from coarser levels."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:372
msgid ""
"When the same grid is reused, this allows sampling intensity to be "
"increased or reduced without replacing all existing locations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:374
msgid ""
"Because sample counts change in steps between grid levels, a level will "
"not usually match the requested count exactly. Within a stratified "
"design, SEPAL selects a level separately for each stratum to approach its"
" allocated count. The result combines stratification with systematic "
"placement; it is not the same as applying one uniform grid across the "
"AOI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:376
msgid "Choose a **Sample size strategy**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:378
msgid "Systematic sample size strategies"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:383
msgid "Result"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:384
msgid "Statistical consideration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:385
msgid "**Oversample**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:386
msgid "Selects a grid level intended to produce at least the requested count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:387
msgid ""
"A successful export can exceed the requested count. If the densest "
"permitted grid still cannot reach the request, the export stops with an "
"error rather than returning fewer samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:388
msgid "**Closest**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:389
msgid "Selects the available grid level closest to the requested count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:390
msgid ""
"The final count may be above or below the request, but it must meet the "
"minimum requirement per stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:391
msgid "**Exact**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:392
msgid ""
"For each stratum, selects the least dense grid level that meets or "
"exceeds the allocated count, then randomly removes excess locations in "
"that stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:393
msgid ""
"The result is not a purely systematic sample. If enough systematic "
"locations cannot be found, the export stops with an error rather than "
"returning fewer samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:395
msgid "Choose a **Grid start**:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:397
msgid ""
"**Random**: derive the global grid start from the seed. Use this when the"
" systematic design requires a randomized start."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:398
msgid ""
"**Fixed**: use the same fixed global start. This is useful for "
"operational consistency, but it does not randomize the grid start."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:400
msgid ""
"Nearby locations are often more alike than locations farther apart. This "
"is called spatial autocorrelation. Samples placed too close together may "
"repeat much of the same information and improve precision less than "
"samples spread farther apart. Use **Min distance** to keep systematic "
"samples at least the chosen distance apart:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:402
msgid ""
"For a stratified design, leave the field empty to use twice the "
"stratification pixel size. An entered value must be at least this large."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:403
msgid ""
"For an unstratified design, leave the field empty to apply no additional "
"minimum-distance constraint."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:410
msgid ""
"A larger minimum distance can reduce the number of locations available in"
" small or fragmented strata."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:413
msgid "Export"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:417
msgid ""
"Some sampling designs use temporary Earth Engine table assets before "
"producing the final output. When temporary assets are required, the "
"design needs a linked Earth Engine account with an asset root and "
"sufficient asset quota, even when the final result is exported to the "
"SEPAL workspace. Without a linked account, SEPAL can use its service "
"account only for designs that do not require temporary assets, and the "
"result can be exported only to the SEPAL workspace. See "
":doc:`../setup/gee` for information about linking an Earth Engine account"
" and configuring an asset root."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:419
msgid ""
"The retrieve button remains disabled until the design is ready to export,"
" including any linked-account and asset-root requirements. Hover over it "
"to view the first unresolved problem."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:421
msgid ""
"Select :btn:`<fa-solid fa-cloud-arrow-down>` to open the **Retrieve** "
"panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:424
msgid "Choose a destination"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:426
msgid ""
"**GEE asset**: available when an Earth Engine account is linked. Export "
"an Earth Engine table asset and enter the asset ID and sharing or "
"overwrite settings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:427
msgid ""
"**SEPAL workspace**: available with a linked account or, for designs that"
" do not require temporary assets, with the SEPAL service account. Export "
"a file in CSV, GeoJSON, KML, KMZ or Shapefile format."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:429
msgid "Select **Retrieve** to start the export task."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:432
msgid "When too few sample locations are found"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:434
msgid ""
"An allocation can pass the panel checks but still request more eligible "
"locations than a stratum can supply. A stratum may be very small, "
"fragmented or mostly masked, or systematic spacing may leave too few grid"
" locations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:436
msgid ""
"During export, SEPAL checks whether it can produce the count allocated to"
" each stratum. If it cannot, the task stops and no output is published. "
"**Closest** is the exception: it may accept the nearest available count "
"above or below the allocation when the minimum requirement is met."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:438
msgid "To inspect a sampling shortage:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:440
msgid "Open **Tasks** in the lower-left corner."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:441
msgid "Select the failed task to open **Task Details**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:442
msgid "Read the **Progress** section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:443
msgid ""
"Review the affected strata, the number of locations found, the missed "
"requirement and the suggestions for the current configuration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:445
msgid ""
"Use the configuration-aware suggestions in **Progress** to revise the "
"sample allocation, arrangement, stratification or AOI. Every stratum must"
" still receive at least two locations, or the higher minimum set with "
"**Min samples/stratum**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:447
msgid ""
"If Earth Engine times out or runs out of memory, the failure is not a "
"sampling shortage. To reduce the workload, use a stored GEE image asset "
"instead of processing a source **Recipe**. If a stratification or "
"anticipated-proportion calculation failed, use a coarser (larger) "
"**Scale** in that panel. If the sample export failed, reducing the "
"allocated sample counts may help. After any change, confirm that the "
"sampling design still supports the study objective."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:450
msgid "Output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:452
msgid ""
"Every output feature is a point with a sample identifier and stratum "
"value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:455
msgid "GEE table asset"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:457
msgid "Each sample point in a GEE table asset includes these core properties:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:459
msgid ":code:`id`: sample identifier;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:460
msgid ":code:`stratum`: numeric stratum value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:462
msgid ""
"The table asset stores the allocation, final counts, labels and colours "
"as collection-level metadata. When you add an exported Sampling Design "
"GEE table asset to a recipe map, SEPAL reads the "
":code:`stratum_class_values`, :code:`stratum_class_palette` and "
":code:`stratum_class_names` properties and uses them to style the points "
"by stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:465
msgid "View the exported points"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:467
msgid "After the GEE table export is complete:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:469
msgid ""
"Open the :btn:`<fa-solid fa-layer-group>` **Layers** panel from the map "
"toolbar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:470
msgid "Select **Add** > **Add an Earth Engine asset**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:471
msgid "Select the exported table asset."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:472
msgid "Add the asset and enable its feature layer for the required map area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:474
msgid ""
"Open **Layer options** to adjust the styling or filter the displayed "
"features."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:476
msgid ""
"A sample near a class boundary can appear to sit in the neighbouring "
"class. Maps are drawn in the Web Mercator projection, which is rarely the"
" projection a stratification is stored in, so the classes are redrawn "
"onto the map's own grid before they are displayed. Near a boundary the "
"class drawn under a point can differ from the one recorded for it. The "
"sample location itself is exact, and the stratum recorded for it is the "
"class found at that location when the design was created."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:478
msgid ""
"Zooming in reduces the effect but does not remove it. If samples appear "
"clearly misplaced rather than marginally, the Scale may be coarser than "
"the detail in the source image. See `Grids and projections`_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:481
msgid "SEPAL workspace file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:483
msgid ""
"Workspace exports repeat design information on each row. Important fields"
" include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:485
msgid "Workspace output fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:489
msgid "Field"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:490
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:491
msgid ":code:`id`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:492
msgid "Sample identifier."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:493
msgid ":code:`stratum`, :code:`label`, :code:`color`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:494
msgid "Stratum value and display information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:495
msgid ":code:`stratumArea`, :code:`totalArea`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:496
msgid "Mapped areas in square metres."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:497
msgid ":code:`stratumWeight`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:498
msgid "Stratum area divided by total mapped area."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:499
msgid ":code:`requestedSampleSize`, :code:`actualSampleSize`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:500
msgid "Planned and exported counts for the stratum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:501
msgid ":code:`sampleExpansionArea`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:502
msgid "Stratum area divided by the actual number of exported samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:503
msgid ":code:`sampleWeight`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:504
msgid "Stratum weight divided by the actual number of exported samples."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:505
msgid ":code:`.geo`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:506
msgid "Point geometry in formats that support this Earth Engine export field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:508
msgid ""
"The :code:`sampleExpansionArea` and :code:`sampleWeight` fields are "
"convenience values derived from the mapped stratum area or weight and the"
" actual exported count: :code:`sampleExpansionArea` is "
":code:`stratumArea` divided by :code:`actualSampleSize`, and "
":code:`sampleWeight` is :code:`stratumWeight` divided by "
":code:`actualSampleSize`. These fields do not prescribe how final results"
" should be calculated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:510
msgid ""
"Retain :code:`actualSampleSize` as the final exported count when it "
"differs from the requested count."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:513
msgid "Grids and projections"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:515
msgid ""
"These settings are advanced. The defaults suit most designs, and a "
"sampling design can be completed without changing them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:517
msgid ""
"SEPAL performs its calculations on a grid of pixels. A grid is defined by"
" a projection, which describes how the curved Earth is represented on a "
"flat map, and a pixel size in metres."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:519
msgid "Sampling Design uses two grids for different purposes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:521
msgid ""
"The **stratification grid** determines how the stratification is read. It"
" decides which stratum each location belongs to, the area and weight of "
"each stratum, and the anticipated proportions. Set it with the **CRS** "
"and **Scale** fields in the **Stratification** panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:522
msgid ""
"The **sample placement grid** determines where samples can be placed. Set"
" its **CRS** in the **Sample Arrangement** panel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:525
msgid "The stratification grid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:527
msgid ""
"When the stratification is a single GEE image asset, SEPAL reads the "
"image on its own grid. **CRS** shows the image's projection, and "
"**Scale** is left empty with the image's pixel size shown in its place. "
"Nothing is redrawn, so each sample records the class that the source "
"image holds at that location."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:529
msgid ""
"Enter a **Scale** to read the stratification at a different resolution. A"
" coarser value processes fewer pixels and calculates faster, but the "
"strata are redrawn onto a grid at that resolution: boundaries between "
"strata can shift slightly, and a location near a boundary may be assigned"
" to a neighbouring stratum. Clear the field to return to the image's own "
"grid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:531
msgid ""
"Changing **CRS** also redraws the strata, because the image's own grid "
"applies only in its own projection. Leave **Scale** blank to keep the "
"detected pixel size, or enter a value to change the resolution too."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:533
msgid ""
"SEPAL also tries to detect a grid for image collections and **Recipe** "
"sources. When a grid can be detected, blank fields use it. When it "
"cannot, blank fields use the defaults shown in the form. Enter a **CRS** "
"or **Scale** only when the design needs a different grid."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:535
msgid ""
"Stratum areas are measured in true square metres whichever grid is used, "
"so areas and weights remain correct."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:538
msgid "The sample placement grid"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:540
msgid ""
"The available projections are equal-area, where every pixel covers the "
"same amount of ground. This prevents some parts of the area of interest "
"from containing more possible sample locations per square kilometre than "
"others. The chosen arrangement and grid start still determine how "
"locations are selected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:542
msgid ""
"**EPSG:6933 — EASE-Grid 2.0 Global**: the default, suitable for most "
"areas."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:543
msgid "**EPSG:6931 — EASE-Grid 2.0 North**: for areas above roughly 60° north."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:544
msgid "**EPSG:6932 — EASE-Grid 2.0 South**: for areas below roughly 60° south."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:546
msgid ""
"The global grid gives every pixel the same area at any latitude, but "
"shapes and spacing become increasingly distorted towards the poles. The "
"polar grids reduce this distortion and provide more even spacing there. "
"What matters is how far north or south the area lies, not how large it "
"is."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:548
msgid ""
"Changing this projection moves the sample locations. It does not change "
"the calculated stratum areas or weights."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/cookbook/sampling_design.rst:550
msgid ""
"An unstratified random design places samples directly within the area of "
"interest and does not use this grid."
msgstr ""

